--TEST--
`new static()` through two levels of inheritance answers the called class, at a static call, an instance call and through `parent::` — `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/void-never-self-static
// `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`'s late static binding, read as a *type* rather than as a dispatch:
// a member declaring `static` answers the class the call named, not the class
// the declaration is written in. Two levels of inheritance, which is where a
// rule that only ever answers "the declaring class" and one that answers "the
// called class" first disagree twice.
class Base {
    public static function make(): static { return new static(); }
    public function chain(): static { return $this; }
    public function name(): string { return "Base"; }
}

class Middle extends Base {
    public function name(): string { return "Middle"; }
}

final class Leaf extends Middle {
    public function name(): string { return "Leaf"; }

    // Reachable only because `Leaf::make()` is typed as a `Leaf`: a `Middle`
    // return would not satisfy this declaration.
    public static function grab(): Leaf { return Leaf::make(); }
}

// The static-call half, at each of the three levels.
Base $b = Base::make();
Middle $m = Middle::make();
Leaf $l = Leaf::make();
echo $b->name(), " ", $m->name(), " ", $l->name(), "\n";

// `parent::` forwards the called class rather than resetting it to the parent,
// which is the whole of "late" in late static binding.
Leaf $grabbed = Leaf::grab();
echo $grabbed->name(), "\n";

// The instance-call half: the receiver's own type is what `static` becomes.
Leaf $chained = $l->chain();
echo $chained->name(), "\n";

// An explicitly named class *sets* the called class, so this is a `Base` and
// owes `Leaf` nothing — the rule is not "always the site's enclosing class".
Base $named = Base::make();
echo $named->name(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Base Middle Leaf
Leaf
Leaf
Base
